```json
{
  "objections": [
    {
      "slide": 1,
      "point": "\"Proven product-market fit\" is inflated: 400 subscribers with no churn or retention data shown doesn't prove PMF.",
      "severity": "high",
      "fix": "Soften to \"early traction\" or present retention/cohort evidence before claiming fit."
    },
    {
      "slide": 5,
      "point": "MRR of 7,200 EUR (~86k EUR ARR) is tiny against a 300k EUR ask; \"unit economics viability\" isn't demonstrated—no margin, burn, or runway.",
      "severity": "high",
      "fix": "Add gross margin, burn rate, runway, and how the roastery changes the economics."
    },
    {
      "slide": null,
      "point": "No churn, CAC, or LTV anywhere. Investors cannot evaluate a subscription business without them.",
      "severity": "high",
      "fix": "Add a metrics slide; if data is incomplete, state honest estimates and how you'll measure them."
    },
    {
      "slide": 7,
      "point": "Use of funds is vague: \"increase capacity\" with no milestones, capacity numbers, or timeline; no equity or terms offered for the 300k.",
      "severity": "high",
      "fix": "Itemize the budget, name operational milestones the roastery unlocks, and state the equity/terms on the table."
    },
    {
      "slide": 3,
      "point": "Competitors are framed as validation, but the deck never says why BeanBox wins against Kyiv City Roast or Idealist—no differentiation or moat.",
      "severity": "high",
      "fix": "Add concrete differentiators (price, quality, sourcing, delivery) and why they're defensible."
    },
    {
      "slide": 2,
      "point": "Premium-pricing evidence comes from restaurant coffee drinks, not bean subscriptions—an evidence mismatch; no TAM or market sizing at all.",
      "severity": "medium",
      "fix": "Use subscription-relevant data or label the restaurant figure as a proxy; add a bottom-up TAM estimate marked as estimate."
    },
    {
      "slide": 4,
      "point": "Traction is attributed to \"strong product quality\"—unsupported; the dossier credits the growth marketer. A 67/month average may also hide deceleration.",
      "severity": "medium",
      "fix": "Attribute growth to marketing execution and show a monthly growth curve instead of a single average."
    },
    {
      "slide": 6,
      "point": "Founder slide is generic job titles: no names, track record, or past results showing why this duo can win a crowded market.",
      "severity": "medium",
      "fix": "Add specific backgrounds, achievements, and why this team beats established roasters."
    }
  ]
}
```
